<!--
  FS.GG.Kit — the coordination kit as ONE versioned package (ADR-0062).

  WHAT THIS REPLACES. Today the coordination kit (the shared skills + the fsgg-coord client + the
  engine tool manifest) is delivered to N receivers by byte-identical file copy: scripts/coordination-sync
  writes it into each `receives: coordination-kit` repo, the coordination-coherence gate fails a
  receiver whose copy drifted, and coordination-propagate opens a rolling sync PR in every receiver on
  each hub change. The report (docs/reports/2026-07-20-*) measured 80-116 sync commits per repo per ten
  days: one hub commit multiplied into N downstream PRs. ADR-0062 Option A ships the kit as a versioned
  artifact instead — receivers REFERENCE it and pick up a change through the Renovate + dispatch fabric
  every other package already uses, exactly as the fsgg-coord CLI already did (ADR-0034 §4.4).

  DERIVED, NOT RESTATED (ADR-0058). This project packs no committed list of kit files. `stage-kit.sh`
  reads the `kit:` rows of registry/repos.yml — the ONE manifest coordination-sync also reads, via the
  same scripts/repos.sh reader — and stages exactly that content-addressed set at pack time. The two
  distribution paths cannot pack different kits while both exist, and a kit row added or retired needs
  no edit here.

  THIS IS PR #1 OF THE MIGRATION (.github#1262). It stands up the producer for the `coordination-kit`
  capability and its materialize contract. It changes NOTHING for receivers yet — the byte-copy fabric
  is untouched and still authoritative — so it is additive and safe to land on its own. The publish
  workflow, the per-receiver switch to a package reference, the `dist/dotnet` build-config half, and the
  deletion of the *-propagate / *-selftest / *-coherence family are the sequenced follow-ups ADR-0062
  names; each is its own reviewable change.

  IDENTITY IS FOREVER (ADR-0003): PackageId FS.GG.Kit is the product identity — never renamed in place.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- A content-only package: it ships kit files + a materialize target, no assembly. A TFM is still
         required for `dotnet pack`; net10.0 matches the repo's global.json floor. IncludeBuildOutput
         false means no lib/ folder is produced or packed. -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
    <GenerateDependencyFile>false</GenerateDependencyFile>

    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>true</SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>
    <PackageId>FS.GG.Kit</PackageId>
    <!-- 0.16.0 -> 0.17.0 (.github#1710 piece 2, ADR-0067 §8/§3). ONE packed member moves:
         `scripts/skill-view` — `check` grows the two lanes SEVEN RECEIVERS EACH HAND-WROTE, and a
         `selftest` subcommand that demonstrates every lane can fire.
         WHAT THIS REPLACES, MEASURED 2026-07-28. ADR-0067 §9 phase 4 retired each receiver's second
         COMMITTED skill root; §8 forbids trading a loud failure for a quiet one, so all seven shipped
         a replacement alarm in their own retirement change. The result was seven implementations of
         ONE invariant — `scripts/check-skill-view-roots.sh` at 23,050 / 21,353 / 18,346 / 18,277 /
         14,864 bytes on SDD / Audio / Rendering / Game / Governance, `tests/composition/
         skill-view-roots.sh` on Net, `tests/composition/lib/skill-view-roots.sh` on Templates. A 55%
         size spread across files asserting the same thing, all written in one day, by a phase whose
         stated purpose is removing hand-copies. They were not a parallel MECHANISM — `skill-view` was
         already a kit-delivered source and `FsggKitCheckSkillView` already ran in every receiver —
         they were two lanes bolted onto it because `check` did not cover them. Now it does.
         MINOR, not patch, and not major. Not major because the member SET is unchanged (28), every
         destination and mode is unchanged, no materialize property is added or altered, and every
         EXISTING invocation behaves identically — the 36 pre-existing legs of `tests/skill-view/
         run.sh` pass unmodified, and the new lanes are reachable ONLY through new opt-in flags. Not
         patch because it is not the prose repair 0.15.1 was: a receiver ACTS on this, by deleting its
         own alarm and calling `check` with the `receiver-proj` flag instead, which is a different runtime contract
         than 0.16.0 offered.
         THE COLLAPSE RAISES THE BAR RATHER THAN LEVELLING IT (#1710: do not weaken an alarm to make
         the collapse easier). Three ways, each measured:
           * PARTIAL VIEWS are graded per-skill by IDENTITY. All seven hand-copies compared DIRECTORY
             COUNTS (`find … -type d | wc -l`), which passes a view whose skills are named nothing
             like the live root's. `selftest` case "right count, wrong name" is that gap, and it reds.
           * THE DANGLING/ABSENT SPLIT is `classify_root`'s, which has always been correct. Rendering's
             and Governance's copies guard absence with a bare `[[ ! -e ]]`, which FOLLOWS symlinks,
             leaving their dangling branch unreachable and the class misattributed. Fixed by
             construction, not by repair — the same defect FS.GG.Audio#212 paid for at `26d2bb7`.
           * THE TRACKED-VIEW-ROOT LANE is ADOPTED from FS.GG.Net's copy, the only one of the seven
             that had it, and now runs for every receiver. A re-committed view root resolves perfectly
             on Linux and passes every other copy's resolution lane, while being exactly the committed
             symlink ADR-0067 §6 rejects on measurement.
         WHAT IS A PARAMETER RATHER THAN A COPY: the `absent-ok "<why>"` flag. An absent view root stays RED
         by default; a receiver whose alarm runs on a BARE checkout (Audio, Game) must excuse it or
         fire on every green build, while one that generates first (SDD, Rendering, Governance) must
         not. The reason is REQUIRED and printed, because what else catches an absent root DIFFERS per
         receiver and must be measured: on Game the kit's own check reds a required job; on Audio it
         does not, because `kit / coordination-kit` grades `<FsggKitSkillRoots>` bytes only and the
         materialize job is not required. An `absent-ok` with an EMPTY reason is refused.
         NOT DONE HERE, DELIBERATELY: #1710's AC1 — moving `Fsgg<Repo>GenerateSkillView` INTO the kit.
         It is blocked on `scripts/repos-audit.sh`'s #1759 view-root sweep, which reds a receiver that
         declares a view root with no LOCAL target ordered `BeforeTargets="FsggKitCheckSkillView"`; a
         kit-owned target would make all seven legitimately stop declaring one and red the daily
         sweep. Filed separately with that as its first task. No `FsggKit*` target is added or
         reordered in this release, so no receiver's generate wiring changes.
         NOTE, like 0.15.0/0.15.1: this touches a `kit:` CONTENT source in registry/repos.yml, so
         `kit-published-coherence`'s PR arm sees it. -->

    <!-- 0.15.1 -> 0.16.0 (.github#1761, the coord-engine 0.14.0 release). ONE packed member moves:
         `dist/dotnet/.config/dotnet-tools.json` — the engine pin, 0.13.0 -> 0.14.0. It is a hashed
